  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Direct F measurements for verapamil and close analogs are at or above 20%: verapamil fasted 22% (17–29%), SR verapamil 49±14% fasted and 34±12% fed, cirrhosis 52±13%; drotaverine 58.2±18.2%; bevantolol 57% (26–98%) and ~60%; vernakalant 58–71%; ketotifen >50%.",
      "card_ids": ["C15","C06","C13","C03","C01","C09","C22","C14","C16","C02"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Verapamil, the closest structural parent, has complete human intestinal absorption (Fa 98–100%) and high passive permeability (Caco-2 Papp 25.2×10^-6 cm/s; R-verapamil 68.9–156.6×10^-6 cm/s; high pig jejunal P_eff), with P-gp efflux only a minor component and CYP3A4 not reducing permeability.",
      "card_ids": ["C40","C41","C43","C44","C51","C55","C57","C59","C60","C49","C50"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records show verapamil has substantial hepatic first-pass metabolism (HLM CLint 34.2–116 µL/min/mg; hepatocyte CLint 7–9 µl/min/10^6 cells; scaled CLha 16.5 mL/min/kg), consistent with its measured F≈22% rather than with F<20%; imipramine also has high HLM CLint (35–51 µL/min/mg) yet is a high-absorption basic amine, so high CLint alone is not affirmative low-F evidence.",
      "card_ids": ["C61","C63","C64","C66","C67","C65","C69","C70","C71"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The only affirmative low-direction endpoint is gallopamil F=15–23%, but it is internally inconsistent with its L1 96.3% value and is a trimethoxy nitrile analog rather than the query's benzazepinone; it does not overturn the supportive direction.",
      "card_ids": ["C20","C23","C11"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "contradictory",
      "decision_effect": "weakened",
      "card_ids": ["C61","C62","C63","C64","C65","C66","C67","C68","C69","C70","C71"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule itself.",
    "No query-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic-stability data; L6 records are for verapamil, imipramine, and donepezil.",
    "The query's benzazepinone lactam and extra methoxy substitution may alter first-pass metabolism relative to verapamil/gallopamil; this is unmeasured.",
    "The gallopamil low-F value (15–23%) is unresolved against its internally inconsistent L1 96.3% value.",
    "Formulation, dose, prandial state, and IV comparator are unspecified for the query."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high: direct F values for verapamil and close analogs are >=20% (verapamil fasted 22%, SR 34–49%, drotaverine 58%, bevantolol ~57–60%, vernakalant 58–71%), and verapamil has complete absorption and high passive permeability. L6 hepatic-clearance records reveal substantial verapamil first-pass metabolism (HLM CLint 34–116 µL/min/mg; CLha 16.5 mL/min/kg), a negative factor that lowers confidence, but no L6 record establishes F<20% for the query or a close parent; the only direct low (gallopamil 15–23%) is internally inconsistent and less transferable. Confidence is therefore low rather than moderate."
}

